DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The snl(3) library provides an easy way of sending and receiving Netlink
     messages, taking care of serialisation and deserialisation.

   IINNIITTIIAALLIISSAATTIIOONN
     Call ssnnll__iinniitt() with a pointer to the struct snl_state and the desired
     Netlink family to initialise the library instance.  To free the library
     instance, call ssnnll__ffrreeee().

     The library functions are NOT multithread-safe.  If multithreading is de‐
     sired, consider initializing an instance per thread.

   MMEEMMOORRYY AALLLLOOCCAATTIIOONN
     The library uses pre-allocated extendable memory buffers to handle mes‐
     sage parsing.  The typical usage pattern is to allocate the necessary
     data structures during the message parsing or writing process via
     ssnnll__aalllloocczz() and free all allocated data at once using ssnnll__cclleeaarr__llbb() af‐
     ter handling the message.

   CCOOMMPPOOSSIINNGG AANNDD SSEENNDDIINNGG MMEESSSSAAGGEESS
     The library does not currently offer any wrappers for writing netlink
     messages.  Simple request messages can be composed by filling in all
     needed fields directly.  Example for constructing an interface dump re‐
     quest:

             struct {
                     struct nlmsghdr hdr;
                     struct ifinfomsg ifmsg;
             } msg = {
                     .hdr.nlmsg_type = RTM_GETLINK,
                     .hdr.nlmsg_flags = NLM_F_DUMP | NLM_F_REQUEST,
                     .hdr.nlmsg_seq = snl_get_seq(ss),
             };
             msg.hdr.nlmsg_len = sizeof(msg);
     ssnnll__ggeett__sseeqq() can be used to generate a unique message number.  To send
     the resulting message, ssnnll__sseenndd() can be used.

   RREECCEEIIVVIINNGG AANNDD PPAARRSSIINNGG MMEESSSSAAGGEESS
     To receive a message, use ssnnll__rreeaadd__mmeessssaaggee().  Currently, this call is
     blocking.

     The library provides an easy way to convert the message to the pre-de‐
     fined C structure.  For each message type, one needs to define rules,
     converting the protocol header fields and the desired attributes to the
     specified structure.  It can be accomplished by using message parsers.
     Each message parser consists of an array of attribute getters and an ar‐
     ray of header field getters.  The former array needs to be sorted by the
     attribute type.  There is a SSNNLL__VVEERRIIFFYY__PPAARRSSEERRSS() macro to check if the
     order is correct.  SSNNLL__DDEECCLLAARREE__PPAARRSSEERR(_p_a_r_s_e_r___n_a_m_e, _f_a_m_i_l_y _h_e_a_d_e_r _t_y_p_e,
     _s_t_r_u_c_t _s_n_l___f_i_e_l_d___p_a_r_s_e_r_[_], _s_t_r_u_c_t _s_n_l___a_t_t_r___p_a_r_s_e_r_[_]) can be used to cre‐
     ate a new parser.  SSNNLL__DDEECCLLAARREE__AATTTTRR__PPAARRSSEERR(_p_a_r_s_e_r___n_a_m_e, _s_t_r_u_c_t
     _s_n_l___f_i_e_l_d___p_a_r_s_e_r_[_]) can be used to create an attribute-only message
     parser.

     Each attribute getter needs to be embedded in the following structure:

     typedef bool snl_parse_attr_f(struct snl_state *ss, struct nlattr *attr, const void *arg, void *target);
     struct snl_attr_parser {
             uint16_t                type;   /* Attribute type */
             uint16_t                off;    /* field offset in the target structure */
             snl_parse_attr_f        *cb;    /* getter function to call */
             const void              *arg;   /* getter function custom argument */
     };
